<p>Your voice may be your passport, as a certain <a href="https://www.theincomparable.com/topics/sneakers/">Robert Redford picture</a> once asserted, but two macOS apps in our spotlight this week view your voice as a way to get more things done on your Mac. We’re talking transcription apps with two apps that take very different approaches to what you can do with vocal recognition and AI.</p>
<p>But that’s not all for this week. We also look at a tool to help you better enjoy email newsletters, an iPhone app for making your photos sound as good as ever and a multiplatform game that helps you fine-tune your puzzle-solving skills.</p>
<p>Got an app of your own that you’d like to see highlighted? Let us know at <code>apps@sixcolors.com</code> with a brief description of what it does, and we’ll take it from there.</p>
<h2>Vox Dictum</h2>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/voxdictum-useme-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Screenshot of a transcription app showing a summary titled ‘## Summary’ about Geoff Belinfante’s 1977 baseball video project. Left sidebar lists recordings; right shows transcript with speaker labels. A purple ‘Done’ button appears." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>I record a lot of interviews, which means I need to transcribe a lot of interviews, and so far, I’ve found the Apple Intelligence tools built into Apple’s various software platforms to be fairly wanting in this area. So when an app like <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/vox-dictum/id6761995146?mt=12">Vox Dictum</a> promises a better transcription experience, I’m all ears.</p>
<p>Vox Dictum is a macOS app that makes privacy its calling card — all the transcription happens directly on your Mac, as do the AI-powered summary features that you can access with the cost of a subscription. A Pro subscription costs $7.99/month and gets you features like large AI models to download to your Mac plus AI summaries while the $12.99/month Pro+ package adds a speaker recognition feature.</p>
<p>I do think it’s easier to label speakers in VoxDictum than with any of Apple’s built-in features, though transcripts — while fairly accurate — feature line breaks that aren’t necessarily suited to my purposes.</p>
<p>As for the summary feature, I found it to be hit-and-miss: Vox Dictum did a great job highlighting the main points of an interview I conducted for a freelance article, making the summary a good reference tool, but a recorded discussion between me and my wife produced a summary with conflicting dates on some action items. That said, I do like that Vox Dictum’s summary feature lets you designate if the recording is an interview, a meeting, a news bulletin or a podcast, which seems to help focus the summary.</p>
<p>Processing time can take a bit, but if you find yourself needing to transcribe a lot of interviews, Vox Dictum is definitely worth a download so you can see if its base features improve on your current approach.</p>
<h2>Bonfiyah</h2>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/bonfiyah-1-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Dark playback interface with controls, speakers, transcript buttons; light panel shows AI Summary with podcast revival text." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Like Vox Dictum, <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/bonfiyah-ai-conversations/id6766562965">Bonfiyah</a> promises to turn recordings into transcripts, while also recognizing who’s saying what. But transcription is really just a sideshow in this case — the app’s real strength lies in its ability to track your conversations.</p>
<p>Available for the iPhone, iPad and Mac — I tried out the macOS version — Bonfiyah records conversations, meetings and brainstorming sessions. (You can also import audio files into the app.) Besides transcription and speaker identification features, the app also taps into AI to summarize action items that come up in your conversation and who’s responsible for taking care of them. A feature I particularly like is an analysis tool that analyzes how to communicate more effectively with the people you talk to on a regular basis.</p>
<p>Transcription does take place off-device via AssemblyAI, though Bonfiyah deletes that info from its servers after seven days. The app includes privacy features such as marking conversations as privileged.</p>
<p>Bonfiyah comes across as a pretty sophisticated tool that might be overkill for anyone who needs transcripts with more basic summary features. But you can try out all the features to start with for 14 days before you need to commit to either a $24.99/month Pro or $34.99/month Pro AI account for more advanced features.</p>
<h2>Letterboxx</h2>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/letterboxx-2-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Screenshot of a News app: sidebar with headlines; main article mentions Raúl Asencio facing trial for sharing intimate footage with a minor; photo of a man; headline “Alonso explains why Chelsea are targeting senior players.”" data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/letterboxx-newsletter-reader/id6760952345?mt=12">Letterboxx</a> exists under the premise that an email inbox is no place to enjoy a newsletter and a traditional mail client is not a reader app. Inboxes are places for taking action like responding to timely messages, but newsletters are something that you want to spend time diving into — hence the Letterboxx interface, which is designed to use the ample space provided on your Mac.</p>
<p>With Letterboxx, you can redirect newsletters sent to supported email accounts to a dedicated reading space, complete with folders for organizing incoming missives by type. You also get ad-filtering tools, ways to highlight relevant text and even shortcuts for managing your many subscriptions.</p>
<p>You’ll know right away if this app is just right for your newsletter-heavy reading habits or if it’s more management features than you need, but you can confirm with a two-week free trial to Letterboxx before the regular $4.99/monthly subscription rate kicks in.</p>
<h2>Echo Photo Album</h2>
<figure class="pull-right"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/echo_merged_framed-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Two phone screens: left shows a cat video with playback controls and menu; right shows a coastal video with options including Bookmark Video, Edit Photo, tags, and Recorded 0:13." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>I don’t really find much use for Live Photos, the built-in iPhone camera feature that adds a little bit of movement and audio to your images. But <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/echo-photo-album/id6775132496">Echo Photo Album</a> might cause me to reconsider augmenting my photos with its focus entirely on audio.</p>
<p>You can record up to 60 seconds of audio in Echo Photo Album, adding it to a shot you either take through the app or find in your photo library. In testing Echo Photo Album on my iPhone — it’s also compatible with iPads and Apple Watches — I found it worked best adding the sound of my cat purring to seem feline portraits or ocean waves crashing to a coastal landscape shot. I imagine it could also come in handy adding sounds to accompany road trips, baby photos and group get-togethers.</p>
<p>You can download Echo Photo Album for free, with a $4.99 purchases removing a 10-image limit.</p>
<h2>Chess Peace</h2>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/chess-peace-3-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Chess Peace app screenshot: Classic 4×4 board with colored circles and pieces (queen, king, knight, bishop, pawn) connected by lines. Sidebar shows piece icons; bottom buttons: Undo, Hint (2), Retreat, Reset. Timer 3:85." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>All work and no play makes for a dull app profiler, so here’s a game I’ve been playing lately that runs on the Mac as well as on iPhones and iPads. (I’ve been testing the macOS version.) <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/chess-peace/id6760270817">Chess Peace</a> is one of those games that you play to keep your mind active. Best of all, you don’t really need to be a grand master to get something out of the game.</p>
<p>The only thing you need to know about chess to thrive at Chess Peace is how each of the different pieces move around the board. That knowledge lets you position each piece on the board of a Chess Peace puzzle in such a way as to prevent any other piece from capturing them — keeping your rook out of the diagonal attacks of your bishop, for example.</p>
<p>The game serves up a new puzzle every day, with a library of older puzzles for you to play as well. An $8.99 in-app purchases unlocks 26,500 puzzles across five game modes plus custom themes to let you change the look of Chess Peace.</p>
